WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.960
Unlike in live-action cinematography

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.320
lighting in animation is something that's built totally

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.220
from scratch.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.900
In other words while in live-action light is controlled

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.040
sometimes too brilliant effect

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.020
in animation.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.030
Light is created.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.400
Of course these days a lot of animated light is computer-generated meaning that virtual lights can be placed

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.440
in 3D environments and actually play off solid surfaces

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.080
like they would in real life.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.079
Like to control of actual lights this too can be used to beautiful effect

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.499
but today I want to talk about lighting as it was done in traditional hand-drawn Cel animation

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.840
particularly in the groundbreaking anime film by Katsuhiro Otomo

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.860
Akira (1988)

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.860
Now Akira is well known for its painstaking animation.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.100
Every frame of the film was composed

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.240
with the closest attention to detail and color

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.380
and that gives it an

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.600
unmatched richness and soul.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.220
For this reason Akira is also a film

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.880
that's been painstakingly picked over and analyzed by others.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.940
But one thing I haven't heard much about is the way that lighting is used by the filmmakers.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.120
Because I see the film's many lights.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.260
Their different qualities and textures.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.620
As a powerful motif and symbol and a vital element

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.640
of its genius.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.780
"The celluloids and backgrounds must still be photographed

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.040
by the Master camera in technicolor.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.040
Here, the operator puts a Donald Duck celluloid on top of a background

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.080
as the overhead camera exposes one frame of film."

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.040
Like I said, Akira was a film made in

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.800
the Cel Animation style developed in the first half of the 20th century

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.540
which involves drawing and painting onto

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.640
transparent slides and layering them over a background.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.780
Frames composed like this were photographed and strung together

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.320
to create the animation.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.200
This 2D traditional style coupled with the tropes

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.160
of anime

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.280
means that the light in Akira is

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.020
quite specific and pronounced.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.220
The lines between shadow and light are

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.880
distinct and evocative in the same way that

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.600
film noir lighting is.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.440
And like in film noir, light in Akira is...

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.020
intimately connected to the city

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.040
at night.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.920
This for example is the very first shot after the opening credits

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.580
a flickering neon sign

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.500
for the neighborhood bar where Kaneda

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.660
is waiting for his friends.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.060
Nothing could more concisely

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.880
communicate the rundown dystopic feeling

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.960
of the future Tokyo, Otomo is going to present us with

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.400
in the very next shot

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.820
and throughout the film.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.900
Indeed, this is a city as sort it

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.160
romantic and multifarious

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.140
as the lights that slash across its million surfaces to make a delicate hole.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.840
The city where the disenfranchised young

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.620
marked their presence and their personalities

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.840
with the streaks of light that their motorcycles leave behind.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.040
A city where authority is as much

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.660
a blinding spotlight

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.840
as it is a gun or badge.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.520
Otomo has said that he wanted Tokyo itself to be a major character

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.340
in Akira

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.400
and one of the ways he flushes this out is with light.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.240
Particularly,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:52.880
Neon.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.300
Neon has a special significance for both, Tokyo and the Cyberpunk genre.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.260
It is the bitter

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.140
but beautiful light that signifies both the colorful radiance

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.120
and the gaudy consumerism of modernity.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.340
The dark dystopic side of which remains Cyberpunk's

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.300
basic focus.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.440
Tokyo for its part has been a cultural hub for neon signage.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.080
Ever since 1957

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:15.820
when the

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.740
Totsuko company unveiled its giant 30-foot Neon billboard

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.940
in the Ginza district

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.700
revealing its new name

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.240
SONY.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.060
Since then the signs have proliferated

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.520
becoming a signature even

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.100
a attraction of Tokyo's night scape.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.340
And novels like William Gibson's "Neuromancer" films like

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.360
Blade Runner (1982)

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.780
took the vivid and suggestive qualities of Tokyo's Neon

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.700
as their palette for a bleak.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.680
Tech-saturated future.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.880
Akira continues this tradition, but here the neon quality

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.660
infuses all the light sources of the film

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.900
both inside and out.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.840
There's the neon on the buildings

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.740
but there's also the neon bright screens that shine in the characters faces

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.020
and the neon control panels of right primary colors.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:03.200
The spotlights that rove in the sky are like giant neon tubes being waved back and forth.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.700
Machine-gun fire is a barrage of neon streaks.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.840
Even the sunlight shining in through the windows has a bluish neon quality to it.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.840
In neon, Neo Tokyo and its inhabitants aren't so much lit

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.180
as caught.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.820
Caught in this huge incandescent web that serves

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.436
only the mandates of...

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.300
money.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.040
"Capital"

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.960
In that web, government is as impotent as a biker gang made up of disaffected children.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.980
It's funny that industry business is notably absent from the film's plot because

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:37.080
its DNA seems to be in the corners of everything.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.060
In a blink, you might miss a news reader at the beginning of the movie

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.400
shining from another flickering light source.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.460
Reporting riots of unemployed workers after tax reforms.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.840
Maybe this is why Tetsuo and once the protagonist and the antagonist of the film

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:52.740
a boy who gains

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.880
extraordinary psychic power.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.240
So often produces a disruption in the light around him.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.920
Whether it's in his own mind

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.580
or in the form of an explosion

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.100
or in the Aura around him even in the vibrant dancing wavelength

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.140
that his brain patterns create.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.160
Indeed, psychic power

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.540
most fully realize in Tetsuo and his counterpoint,

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:16.480
Akira.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.940
Represent the only kind of power that can have an effect on the state of things in Neo Tokyo.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:25.500
This is perhaps the reason that the protesters turned to Akira

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.700
for their salvation.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.340
Because even when the authorities turned their biggest spotlight on him

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.400
a mega satellite laser that takes the name

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.520
of our son.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:35.820
He comes out...

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:36.700
The Victor.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.480
In the end the salvation represented in Tetsuo

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.620
comes at the cost of Ruin.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.240
A catastrophe which consumes Neo Tokyo

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.740
in the form of a giant ball

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:47.720
of light.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.540
One single uniform white light that erases the countless

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.060
artificial lights of the city.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.920
Otomo seems to suggest that there's some freedom in this

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:56.500
some

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.960
rebirth as true sunlight wipes across the city in patches.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.560
When you have to draw light

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.160
24 times in every second of a two-hour movie

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:12.600
it must be the case that you gain a new appreciation for how it can tell a story of its own.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.460
And that's what Otomo and his crew have done in Akira.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.820
Light is layered into the story

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:18.400
like...

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.740
it's layered into the animation cells.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.340
Pointing toward meaning

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.640
behind the film at the corners of it.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.160
And illuminating from different angles

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.160
the characters

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.460
and their place in this neon world.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:30.560
In other words

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.660
Akira is a great example of how you animate

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.500
Light

